+## 1. Brief
+Prompt: [`prompts/salary-audit-data.md`](prompts/salary-audit-data.md), verbatim, with the lead's three
+follow-ups (new base 2474fc4, fresh data, commit often).
+
+The user asked on 2026-10-11: "Are you sure the salary range of the big tech are correct?! They don't look
+correct and kinda all span too evenly." This lane is the data half of a two-person squad. salary-audit-backend
+traces the pipeline. It checks the **shape** of the ranges across each whole board, which the earlier
+posting-by-posting checks (data check 3, vetting round 2) could not see. It asks whether those ranges are what
+the companies posted, and if not, why.
+
+**Answer.** The numbers beta shows are the companies' own posted figures. The "even" look is real: each of
+these companies publishes fixed-ratio bands per level, and the main site's boards do the same. Three things
+are ours:
+- **Level spans.** Our code shows a posting that prices two levels as one range, from the lower level's
+  minimum to the upper level's maximum, with no mark on the card. This applies to 61% of NVIDIA's paid
+  postings and 10% of Microsoft's (SA-1).
+- **False span claims on Amazon.** On 15% of Amazon's paid postings the card says its range is the span of
+  several places' ranges, but it shows one place's row (SA-2).
+- **Cut pay rows.** Some Amazon pay rows are cut (SA-3, SA-7), against the user's "present them as they are
+  and not cut anything".
+
