+
+**SA-1 (High): several posted levels are shown as one range, with no mark on the card.**
+- What NVIDIA posts: "The base salary range is 184,000 USD - 287,500 USD for Level 4, and 224,000 USD -
+  356,500 USD for Level 5." This is nvidia:893395255693 "Senior System Software Engineer - Performance",
+  https://jobs.nvidia.com/careers/job/893395255693, the board's most common shown range (223 postings).
+- What we show:
+  - the card: "$184K–356K" (`salary.min/max` is the span; `salary.levels` keeps both levels, pinned);
+  - the chart: the span's midpoint, $270,250;
+  - Juice: the same midpoint.
+- None of the 887 NVIDIA and 157 Microsoft shown ranges is a range posted for one level anywhere on the
+  board (`findings.json`). Example: microsoft:1970393556627435 "Principal Software Engineer, CoreAI" shows
+  $142,800–296,400 for IC5 $142,800–274,800 and IC6 $165,600–296,400.
+- The drawer's "Pay by level" line and the chart tip are right. The card, which people read first, is not;
+  a range per place gets "· N places".
+- Fix: the card says "· 2 levels" with the levels in its tip (patch). The span stays, because the posting
+  offers either level. Whether Juice and the chart should use the lower level, or the level the title
+  names, is a lead decision; this audit does not propose it.
+
+**SA-2 (Medium-High): Amazon's "· N places" range is one place's row.**
+- amazon:10371179 "Senior Financial Analyst, Engineering Capex Analytics & Planning",
+  https://www.amazon.jobs/en/jobs/10371179, posts five rows:
+  - New York $73,600–128,800;
+  - Nashville $60,200–105,400;
+  - Irving $66,900–117,100;
+  - Arlington $66,900–117,100;
+  - Bellevue $82,700–117,100.
+- The card reads "$83K–117K · 4 places", with the tip "$83K–117K runs from the lowest to the highest of 4
+  ranges posted for different places". The span is $60K–129K.
+- The chart's ranges view draws the Bellevue bar with the tip "One range over 4 places' ranges".
+- Apple, whose salary is the span, is right.
+- The card fix was written in fix wave 5 and never applied (`fix5-data-display.diff`, which passes
+  `git apply --check` on 2474fc4). The chart tip is in `salary-audit-data-chart.diff`.
+- Alternative for the lead: make Amazon's `salary.min/max` the span in `amazonSalary`, as Apple's is. Then
+  the card, the chart and the sort would all use one range.
+
+**SA-3 (Medium): Amazon pay rows are cut, against "not cut anything".**
+- amazon:10385593 "Operations Engineer", https://www.amazon.jobs/en/jobs/10385593:
+  - the salary is "USA, TX, AUSTIN - 60,700.00 - 85,000.00 USD annually";
+  - the stored `salary.ranges` holds 12 rows, from "USA, AZ, Mesa" to "USA, GA, ATLANTA";
+  - Austin's row, and every row after Georgia, are gone.
+- 57 stored postings have exactly 12 ranges; for 19 of them the posting's own row is not among them.
+- The stored description keeps 3 rows: `trimDescription` keeps 4 pay paragraphs, and the intro sentence is
+  one of them.
+- The served view regroups the stored rows, so no served posting has 12 or more, but the cut rows cannot be
+  recovered. Places without their row are scored, and filtered, on the primary row.
+- Fix: no cap in `amazonSalary` or `deriveSalary` (`salary-audit-data-amazon-rows.diff`), and
+  `fix5-data-trim.diff` (every table row kept). Both need the next crawl to restore the rows.
